One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, which can lead to **Path Traversal** attacks. This occurs when an application uses user-supplied input directly to construct a file path without validating that the input does not contain characters like `../`.

```java
// BAD CODE: Improperly uses user input to access files
public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
    // This method is vulnerable to Path Traversal attacks because it 
    // uses the filename string directly from the user to build a path.
    File file = new File("/var/webapp/uploads/" + filename);

    // If a user passes "../../etc/passwd", they could read sensitive system files
    Path content = Files.readAllLines(file.toPath());
    System.out.println(content);
}
```

***

```java
// GOOD CODE: Implements validation to prevent Path Traversal
public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
    File baseDirectory = new File("/var/webapp/uploads/");
    File file = new File(baseDirectory, filename);

    // The canonical path resolves all ".." and "." sequences
    String canonicalPath = file.getCanonicalPath();

    // Check if the resulting path still resides within the intended directory
    if (!canonicalPath.startsWith(baseDirectory.getCanonicalPath())) {
        throw new SecurityException("Invalid file path: Access denied.");
    }

    // At this point, the path is safely contained within the upload directory
    Path content = Files.readAllLines(file.toPath());
    System.out.println(content);
}
```

